Active Directory fundamentals
Active Directory (AD DS) is the directory that holds users, computers and groups, and domain controllers are the servers that run it.
The structure, using our names:
• Forest — the top-level security boundary. Ours is DOMROOT.INTERNAL. All domains in a forest trust each other automatically.
• Domain — a container for users and computers with shared policies. DOMROOT.INTERNAL is the forest root domain.
• Child domain — a domain under another in the same namespace. USERDOMAIN01.DOMROOT.INTERNAL is a child of DOMROOT.INTERNAL.
• OU (Organisational Unit) — a folder inside a domain used to group objects and apply Group Policy.
• Computer object — the record a VM gets in AD when it is domain joined. No object = not joined.
Domain controllers and their roles:
• Domain Controller (DC) — a server holding a copy of the AD database, answering logons and lookups. DCs replicate changes to each other.
• Writable DC vs RODC — most DCs are writable; a Read-Only DC holds a copy that cannot be changed locally, used in less secure locations.
• Global Catalog (GC) — a DC that also holds a partial copy of every domain in the forest, used for forest-wide searches and logons. P058CLOsvrHMC01 is a GC.
• KDC (Key Distribution Center) — the Kerberos service on every DC that issues tickets.
• FSMO roles (Flexible Single Master Operations) — five jobs only one DC can do at a time:
    ◦ Schema Master and Domain Naming Master — one per forest.
    ◦ PDC Emulator — per domain; time source, password changes, account lockouts. P058CLOsvrHMC01 holds this for USERDOMAIN01.
    ◦ RID Master — per domain; hands out ID pools for new objects.
    ◦ Infrastructure Master — per domain; tracks cross-domain references.
Sites and levels:
• AD Site — a group of well-connected subnets. Clients prefer a DC in their own site. Both our VM and DC are in AZUREUKSOUTH.
• Subnet-to-site mapping — tells AD which subnet belongs to which site. A missing mapping can send clients to a distant or legacy DC.
• Functional level — the feature level of a domain or forest, capped by its oldest DC OS. Retiring legacy DCs often lets it be raised.
• DC decommissioning (demotion) — removing the DC role properly, which cleans its DNS records and metadata. A DC switched off without demotion leaves stale records behind.
What "legacy DC" usually means: a DC on an old OS (2008/2012 R2), or DCs in the old on-premises datacentre being retired in favour of Azure DCs. Clarify which one Sam means.
DNS and how AD depends on it
AD cannot work without DNS: clients find DCs by asking DNS, so a legacy DC reference very often lives in DNS.
How a VM finds its DC:
1. The VM reads its DNS server list (from the NIC or the VNet).
2. It asks those servers for SRV records such as _ldap._tcp.dc._msdcs.USERDOMAIN01.DOMROOT.INTERNAL.
3. DNS returns the list of DCs registered for the domain, preferring those in the VM's AD site.
4. The VM contacts a DC (the DC Locator process) and caches it. nltest /dsgetdc shows the result.
Record types to know:
• A record — name to IPv4 address.
• PTR record — IP back to name (reverse lookup). Missing for 10.186.129.166.
• SRV record — advertises a service (LDAP, Kerberos, GC) and which servers provide it. DCs register these automatically via Netlogon.
• CNAME — an alias pointing one name at another.
• SOA / NS — say which servers are authoritative for a zone.
Zones and forwarding:
• Zone — the set of records a DNS server is responsible for, e.g. DOMROOT.INTERNAL.
• AD-integrated zone — a zone stored in AD and replicated between DCs. Stale DC records survive here if a DC was not demoted cleanly.
• _msdcs zone — the forest-wide zone holding DC locator records. A key place to look for legacy DC entries.
• Forwarder — where a DNS server sends queries it cannot answer itself.
• Conditional forwarder — "send queries for this domain to these IPs". If it still lists legacy DC IPs, lookups go to old servers.
• Recursive vs authoritative — a resolver chases the answer for you; an authoritative server owns the zone.
• Scavenging — automatic cleanup of stale dynamic records.
• TTL — how long an answer is cached before being asked again.
Azure-specific DNS:
• 168.63.129.16 — Azure's built-in platform IP: DHCP, Azure-provided DNS and health probes. Seen as our VM's DHCP server; normal.
• reddog.microsoft.com — Azure's default DNS suffix on VMs. Normal.
• Custom DNS servers — set on the VNet or NIC to point VMs at your own DNS (DCs or resolvers) instead of Azure DNS. Ours point at the three .166 addresses.
• Azure Private DNS Resolver — managed service with inbound and outbound endpoints and forwarding rulesets. Rulesets can hold conditional forwards to DC IPs.
• Azure Private DNS zones — zones for private names, e.g. privatelink zones for Private Endpoints.
• Azure Firewall DNS proxy — the firewall acts as the DNS server for VMs and forwards on to configured servers.
Likely question for Neal: are the .166 addresses resolvers, firewall DNS proxies or DCs, and do they or their forwarding rules still send queries to legacy DC IPs?
Azure networking
Neal will think in terms of VNets, routes, firewalls and DNS settings; these are the terms he is likely to use.
Building blocks:
• VNet (Virtual Network) — a private network in Azure with an address space, e.g. 10.186.0.0/16.
• Subnet — a slice of a VNet. Our VM is in 10.186.29.0/24 (mask 255.255.255.0, gateway 10.186.29.1).
• CIDR notation — /24 = 256 addresses, /16 = 65,536. Azure reserves 5 addresses per subnet.
• NIC (Network Interface) — the VM's network card in Azure; holds the private IP and optionally its own DNS servers.
• Private IP / static vs dynamic — DCs should have static private IPs so DNS settings stay valid.
Topology:
• Hub-and-spoke — a central hub VNet with shared services (firewall, DNS, DCs, gateways); spoke VNets for workloads peer to it.
• Landing zone — the standard, governed Azure environment (subscriptions, policies, networking) workloads deploy into.
• VNet peering — connects two VNets privately. Not transitive: spoke A cannot reach spoke B through the hub without routing via a firewall or NVA.
• Gateway transit — lets spokes use the hub's VPN/ExpressRoute gateway.
• ExpressRoute / Site-to-site VPN — private links to on-premises; how Azure VMs would reach legacy on-prem DCs.
Traffic control:
• NSG (Network Security Group) — allow/deny rules on a subnet or NIC by IP, port and protocol.
• UDR (User-Defined Route) / route table — overrides default routing, e.g. sends 0.0.0.0/0 to the firewall.
• Azure Firewall / NVA — central firewall inspecting traffic between spokes and to on-prem. Firewall rules or DNS proxy settings can still name legacy DC IPs.
• ASG (Application Security Group) — groups NICs so NSG rules can refer to roles, not IPs.
• Private Endpoint — a private IP for a PaaS service inside your VNet.
DNS settings in Azure (where old IPs hide):
• VNet DNS servers — apply to every VM in the VNet. Portal: VNet → Settings → DNS servers.
• NIC DNS servers — override the VNet setting for one VM. Portal: NIC → DNS servers.
• A change to VNet DNS only reaches a VM after a DHCP renewal or reboot, so VMs can run on old settings for a long time.
