If these are blocked between a VM and its DC, domain logon and NLA fail.
Authentication and access
These terms explain why your RDP worked on one VM and failed on the SHIR VM.
• Domain join — adding a computer to a domain: it gets a computer object and a machine password, and starts trusting the domain's DCs.
• Workgroup / standalone — a machine not in any domain; only its local accounts can log on. The SHIR VM behaves like this.
• Secure channel — the trusted link between a joined computer and the domain, using the machine password. Check with nltest /sc_query:<domain>. A broken channel gives "trust relationship failed" errors.
• Kerberos — the default domain authentication protocol. The client gets a ticket from the KDC and presents it to the server. Needs DNS names and time within 5 minutes.
• NTLM — older challenge/response protocol, used when Kerberos is not possible (e.g. connecting by IP). Being phased out for security.
• NLA (Network Level Authentication) — RDP checks your credentials before opening the session. It needs to reach a DC for domain accounts, which is why the SHIR VM rejected you.
• CredSSP — the protocol RDP uses to pass credentials securely for NLA.
• GPO (Group Policy Object) — settings pushed from AD to joined machines, e.g. adding your team's group to Remote Desktop Users. This is how you normally get RDP access.
• Restricted Groups / Group Policy Preferences — GPO features that control local group membership.
• Local admin account — the built-in account created at VM deployment; works without a domain. Log on as .\username.
• LAPS (Local Administrator Password Solution) — rotates local admin passwords and stores them in AD or Entra ID.
• Service account / gMSA — accounts applications run as. A gMSA (group Managed Service Account) has a password AD rotates automatically.
• LDAP bind — an application authenticating to a DC to read the directory. Apps often hard-code a DC name or IP for this — a classic legacy reference.
• Entra ID (formerly Azure AD) — Microsoft's cloud identity service. Different from AD DS: Azure portal logins use Entra ID; VM domain logons use AD DS.
• Entra Connect — syncs AD users into Entra ID.
• Entra Domain Services — Microsoft-managed AD DS in Azure (not what we have; ours are self-managed DC VMs).
• Time sync (W32Time) — DCs sync time from the PDC emulator; Kerberos breaks if clocks drift.
Where legacy DC references hide
Old DC references usually survive in DNS settings and in application config, not in the DC Locator result itself. Use this as a shared checklist on the call.
