**10. Learn the common ports**

You do not need to memorise every port immediately. Start with these:

| Service             |  Common destination port | Purpose                                      |
| ------------------- | -----------------------: | -------------------------------------------- |
| DNS                 |               TCP/UDP 53 | Name resolution                              |
| Kerberos            |               TCP/UDP 88 | Authentication                               |
| LDAP / DC discovery |              TCP/UDP 389 | Directory access and discovery functions     |
| LDAPS               |                  TCP 636 | LDAP over TLS                                |
| SMB                 |                  TCP 445 | File access and Windows domain functions     |
| RPC endpoint mapper |                  TCP 135 | Locating RPC services                        |
| Dynamic RPC         | Commonly TCP 49152–65535 | Various Windows/AD operations                |
| Global Catalog      |          TCP 3268 / 3269 | Forest-wide directory queries; 3269 uses TLS |
| NTP                 |                  UDP 123 | Time synchronisation                         |
| HTTPS               |                  TCP 443 | Web/API communication                        |
| RDP                 |    Commonly TCP/UDP 3389 | Remote desktop                               |

Actual requirements depend on the operation and configuration. This is a learning reference, not a list of ports to open automatically. Microsoft provides detailed AD firewall requirements. ([Microsoft Learn][10])

**Why time matters:** Kerberos authentication is sensitive to clock differences. A login issue can involve time synchronisation as well as credentials or networking.

