**11. Practise these checks and understand their limits**

Run these on a VM you are authorised to investigate. The examples inspect settings or make diagnostic queries; they do not repair configuration.

**A. Identify the machine and your current identity**

```cmd
hostname
whoami
ipconfig /all
```

Record the hostname, IP, subnet, gateway and DNS servers.

**B. Confirm domain membership**

```powershell
Get-CimInstance Win32_ComputerSystem |
    Select-Object Name, Domain, PartOfDomain
```

**C. Discover a domain controller**

```cmd
nltest /dsgetdc:USERDOMAIN01.DOMROOT.INTERNAL
```

**D. Query domain service records**

```cmd
nslookup -type=SRV _ldap._tcp.dc._msdcs.USERDOMAIN01.DOMROOT.INTERNAL
```

This helps show the DC discovery records returned by DNS.

**E. Compare DNS responses from a specific resolver**

```cmd
nslookup -type=SRV _ldap._tcp.dc._msdcs.USERDOMAIN01.DOMROOT.INTERNAL 10.186.129.166
```

The final address selects the DNS server to query. ([Microsoft Learn][11])

**F. Test a TCP connection**

```powershell
Test-NetConnection 10.186.255.139 -Port 389
```

`TcpTestSucceeded = True` means the TCP connection succeeded. **It does not prove an LDAP bind or authentication succeeded**, and this command does not test UDP. ([Microsoft Learn][12])

**G. Inspect routes**

```cmd
route print
```

This shows guest Windows routes. Azure effective routes provide additional information about the Azure network path.

**Avoid these common conclusions**

| Finding                   | Accurate interpretation                                                 |
| ------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Ping succeeds             | ICMP communication succeeded                                            |
| Ping fails                | ICMP failed; the destination or application may still be available      |
| TCP port succeeds         | A TCP connection succeeded to that address and port                     |
| DNS resolves              | DNS returned an answer; verify it is the intended answer                |
| Reverse lookup fails      | The reverse lookup failed; forward lookup may still work                |
| `nltest` returns a new DC | DC discovery returned that DC; applications may have other dependencies |
| VM is not domain joined   | No AD machine membership; application dependencies still need checking  |

